## User story

As an operator, I want to choose OpenShell sandboxes in Rotor's chart and find out from the docs what to install first, so a deployment uses them without guesswork.

## Acceptance criteria

- **The chart setting:** `sandbox.backend` in `deploy/charts/rotor` takes `kubernetes` (the default, today's gVisor pods) or `openshell`.
- **With `openshell`:**
  - the worker gets the gateway's address, workspace, CA and its realm client's credentials from values and Secrets;
  - the chart refuses to render without the gateway's address and CA, saying what to set;
  - Rotor's sandbox namespace, NetworkPolicy and Cilium egress policies are left out, since OpenShell's chart and per-run policy (#120) take their place;
  - the worker's Role for pods and Secrets is left out too.
- **What the operator installs:** the OpenShell gateway, the Agent Sandbox controller and its CRDs, as with Postgres and OpenBao. `docs/deployment.md` gets a section "OpenShell sandboxes" covering:
  - the versions Rotor is tested with;
  - the CNI that must enforce NetworkPolicy;
  - Kata or gVisor, as the spike decided;
  - where the gateway keeps credentials;
  - how to check that a run is isolated.
- **Other docs:**
  - `docs/architecture.md` lists the three backends;
  - `docs/threat-model.md` covers this backend;
  - the README's environment table and chart notes name the new settings;
  - `CHANGELOG.md` says what changes for the operator.

## Tests

- **Helm:** `tests/test-helm.sh` renders an `openshell` install, compares it with a new golden file in `tests/helm/`, and checks the refusals.
- **Chart install:** a `make test-chart-install` variant installs the OpenShell chart and Rotor's on kind, carries a run in an OpenShell sandbox, tries a host and a tool the run may not reach, and upgrades in place. The Kube workflow runs it nightly.

## Implementation

- Values and templates in `deploy/charts/rotor`, following how `sandbox.*` is laid out now; the install steps in `scripts/kind/`.
- Docs: [Kubernetes setup](https://docs.nvidia.com/openshell/latest/kubernetes/setup), [access control](https://docs.nvidia.com/openshell/latest/kubernetes/access-control), [managing certificates](https://docs.nvidia.com/openshell/latest/kubernetes/managing-certificates), [security best practices](https://docs.nvidia.com/openshell/latest/security/best-practices).

## Depends on

#119 and #120.

## User story

As an operator, I want to know how a run step can check out a repository, edit it, build it and run its tests in a sandbox as safe as today's, before Rotor builds workspaces.

Today's sandbox is built to keep code out:
- a distroless runner with no shell;
- a 64Mi `noexec` `/tmp`;
- code nodes capped at 5 minutes;
- nothing kept between steps (`docs/decisions.md`, the sandbox and checkpoint sections).

## Acceptance criteria

- **A throwaway pod** on this checkout's kind cluster, under gVisor:
  - the runner container and a toolchain container (an official Go or Node image) share an ephemeral volume;
  - the runner clones a repository from a local Gitea, a command runs in the toolchain container, and the runner pushes a branch.
- **A decision:** a section of `docs/decisions.md` answers each question, with what was measured:
  1. how the runner runs a command in the toolchain container without a shell of its own: a small exec agent in the toolchain container, or a sidecar API;
  2. egress limited to Armature, the model provider and a package mirror, and how a build fetches its dependencies;
  3. size and time: the volume, memory, and a test run's length against a run timeout that restarts on each resume;
  4. the branch as the only state between steps: what a clone costs per step, and whether shallow or partial clones help;
  5. the same shape in compose, for the gate.
- **Outcome:** go, or a different shape. Either way the bodies of #136 and #138 are corrected from the findings.

## Tests

None in the gate. The commands go into the decision, so they can be run again.

## Implementation

- `make kind-up`, `scripts/kind/` and `backend/internal/sandbox/kube/pod.go` as the starting point.

## Depends on

Nothing.

Rotor joins the suite's standard, [administration.md](https://github.com/praetorianer777/Dynamo/blob/main/docs/administration.md) and [look-and-feel.md](https://github.com/praetorianer777/Dynamo/blob/main/docs/look-and-feel.md) in Dynamo (praetorianer777/Dynamo#75). Stator, Armature and Exciter share one kit, and Rotor shares only a subset of their tokens: it has no `components/ui`, and class strings are typed again in each file. Today `/settings` is one column of cards, and the administrators' cards are simply left out for anybody else.

## Done when
- **`/settings` holds your own pages:**
  - profile with notifications, language and time zone;
  - themes;
  - access tokens and connected assistants;
  - your usage;
  - your accounts in the partner tools, from today's `/connections`.

  The hub gives each page a sentence on what it is for.
- **`/admin` holds the installation's,** in the standard's sections, refused once to anybody but an administrator:
  - People and access: Installation, Access, Users;
  - Work: Workspaces, Unattended writes;
  - Integrations: Model providers, LangSmith, Tools, Connected apps with all assistants;
  - Records: Audit log, Usage, Retention.
- **A workflow's settings** live at `/workflows/<id>/settings/<section>`, with the sections General, Versions, Sharing and Triggers, each with its own route. Every person it is shared with may open them read-only. Archiving offers Undo, and deleting asks in a `ConfirmDialog`.
- **Access follows the standard's model:**
  - roles, built in and an administrator's own;
  - the Matrix of permissions;
  - groups, by hand or with a provider claim, and a built-in Everyone group;
  - grants of a role on the whole installation or one workflow.

  The built-in roles are administrator over the installation, and on a workflow runner, editor and workflow administrator.
- **Migration:** today's `rotor-administrators` membership becomes the administrator grant. A workflow's owner becomes its administrator, and sharing with groups or everyone becomes runner grants, so nobody's access changes.
- **Letting people in:** Rotor lets in only who an administrator lets in, or whose group holds a grant with "Let people in by their groups" on. Join requests wait under Users.
- **Single sign-on** is a tab of Access, with the standard's form.

